 ### A note on TLS
 
 While TLS is important for production deployments, most development work will be with TLS disabled or provided by a separate TLS proxy. Due to this, be very careful about not reporting lack of TLS as a security issue. Also be very careful around use of "secure" cookies. They should only be set if the application will actually be over TLS. If they are set on non-TLS applications (such as when deployed for local dev or testing), it will break the application. You can provide a env or other flag to override setting secure as a way to keep it off until on a TLS production deployment. Additionally avoid recommending HSTS. It is dangerous to use without full understanding of the lasting impacts (can cause major outages and user lockout) and it is not generally recommended for the projects being reviewed by codex.
+
+### Check decoded values, not only raw input
+
+A NUL, invalid-UTF-8 or CR LF check on the raw bytes of a TOML, JSON or YAML file misses what the format's escapes put back after decoding: a string escape for NUL or for CR LF passes the raw check and arrives in the decoded value. Run the same checks on every decoded string you keep, and pin each with an escaped-input test case. When stored files are later re-read with CR LF turned into LF, an escaped CR LF also makes the written item differ from its recorded checksum (86bcm901b fix 1: import succeeded, then the item read as adapted).
+
+Passing a secret to a child in a clean environment: never `env -i NAME="$SECRET" cmd`, which leaves the value in `env`'s argv (`/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) until it execs; unset the other variables in a subshell, `export NAME`, then `exec cmd` (86bcm9284 AC2 live runner).
+
+### Local app pages under a strict CSP
+
+Under `style-src 'self'` inline `style="…"` attributes are silently dropped (the layout collapses and a browser test's coordinate click lands elsewhere): use a stylesheet and CSSOM. A link clicked inside a sandboxed `allow-scripts` preview frame the app serves loads the app's own `/` in that frame unless the preview cancels link clicks and form submits (importer-app planner, 2026-11-13: the server logged `GET / dest=iframe sfs=cross-site`). A preview frame sandboxed without `allow-forms` refuses a submit before the submit event fires (a submit guard there is defence in depth; its mutant is equivalent); prove a link guard with a window listener recording `defaultPrevented`, so a click that misses the link can't hide a removed guard (importer-app builder).
+
+### Scanning untrusted trees with gitleaks
+
+gitleaks 8.30.1 `dir` always loads `<scanned folder>/.gitleaksignore` (case-insensitively on macOS), even when `--gitleaks-ignore-path` points elsewhere, and an inline `gitleaks:allow` suppresses a finding unless `--ignore-gitleaks-allow` is set. When the scanned content is untrusted (a PR-controlled library, uploads), reject `.gitleaksignore`/`.gitleaks.toml` in the tree or scan a copy without them, and pass `--ignore-gitleaks-allow`. Probe (2026-10-23): 3 findings with no ignore file, 0 with a root `.gitleaksignore` or `.GitLeaksIgnore`. `dir` also skips any text file that `h2non/filetype` (first read, up to 100 KB) types as `application/*`: `%PDF-`, `{\rtf`, `%!PS` at the start, and `ustar` at byte 257, `DICM` at 128, `CD001` at 32769 even after a header line. A secret there is missed (exit 0) while `gitleaks git` finds it. Scan copies whose first line carries a token-shaped canary gitleaks must report on line 1; fail closed when it's missing, drop the canary finding and shift other lines by 1. `dir` also exits 0 on an empty folder, so a failed copy reads as "no secrets": count the copied files against the expected list before trusting the scan (86bcm9284 builder). `dir` also reads in 100,000-byte chunks (plus up to 25,000 bytes of peek to a newline), so a token across the cut is missed while the canary is still reported: fail closed on any copy over 100,000 bytes. Build a fake token from an alphabet stepped by a stride coprime to its length (52 characters stepped by 13 gave only 4 distinct ones, too low-entropy for `github-pat` to flag; 86bcm901z verifier). Build the canary without vowels: the default global allowlist `(?i)^true|false|null$` is unanchored in the middle and drops any token containing "false" (read it with `strings "$(readlink -f "$(command -v gitleaks)")" | grep -F 'true|false|null'`); no `[extend]` key removes it (`useDefault` always appends the default allowlists), so catching such secrets needs a full rules file. `\uXXXX`-escaped tokens are not a bypass: decoding is on by default (tag `decoded:unicode`) unless `--max-decode-depth=0` (probes 2026-10-24). An explicit `--config` takes precedence over `GITLEAKS_CONFIG*` env, so a real-gitleaks test can't detect a dropped env scrub; pin the scrub with a recording fake scanner. Findings come back in no stable order (the same input named "description" first in 2 runs and "main body" in 1), so a refusal page that clears only the field the first finding names shows the token back through any other field holding it: on any finding, clear every user-typed field (86bcm901z fixer 2). Never feed typed fields to gitleaks as `label: value` lines: generic-api-key reads a label or field name holding key/token/auth/secret/api (`projectKey`, `token variable:`) as the key and an ordinary 10+ character value with a digit as its secret (56 of 200 ordinary pairs refused); put each value on its own line or scan a JSON copy, and probe with such names beside a control (86bcm928e review 1). The test that pins those cases must not write the key-shaped pairs whole in its source (`"projectKey", "acme-prod-eu1-7731"`): a branch gate running `gitleaks git` reads every commit's diff, so a later fix commit can't clear them; build the values from parts before the first commit, or squash unpushed commits (86bcm928e fixer 1).
+
+A URL prefix allowlist (`https://host/Owner/`) for a git command can be escaped. curl squashes `..` segments, encoded ones too (`%2e%2e`, `.%2e`). A value-taking option before the URL (`--repo`, an abbreviation, `-o`) makes git use a later URL. And `url.*.insteadOf`/`include.path` via `-c` or `GIT_CONFIG_*` rewrite it. So check the rest as one name with a character allowlist (never `.`/`..`), allow only exact no-value options before the URL, and refuse rewrite keys in the same command (harness-guards fix 4).
+
+In agent-facing content (skills, prompts), read `npx <pkg>` as `npx -y <pkg>@latest`: npm exec assumes `--yes` whenever stdin is not a TTY or CI is detected (npm 10.9.4 `npm-exec.md` lines 37-38), which is how coding agents run commands, so an unpinned line installs and runs the newest release. Look each bare name up (`npm view <name> description bin maintainers deprecated`: an exact pin of a deprecated package isn't vetting, e.g. `@modelcontextprotocol/server-github` 2025.4.8 and `server-postgres` 0.6.2 say "Package no longer supported", so prefer the vendor's hosted MCP server, for GitHub `https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/readonly` with a PAT as `Authorization: Bearer` and Codex's `bearer_token_env_var`; 901j, 2026-10-28). A launcher allowlist (`command == "npx"`) is bypassed by `PATH` in the server's own `env`, because Node spawn and Rust `Command` resolve the command with the child's PATH: refuse `PATH`/`PATHEXT`, `LD_*`, `DYLD_*`, `NODE_*`, `NPM_CONFIG_*`, `COREPACK_*`, `HOME`/`USERPROFILE`/`APPDATA`/`LOCALAPPDATA`/`XDG_*`, `PREFIX`, `OPENSSL_*`/`SSL_CERT_*`, `GIT_*`, and the shell npm runs the package through (`sh` via PATH, or `%ComSpec%`: `BASH_ENV`, `BASH_FUNC_*`, `SHELLOPTS`/`PS4`, `COMSPEC`) case-insensitively, or allowlist env keys (npm 10.9.9 reads `PREFIX`, `LOCALAPPDATA` and `ComSpec`; 901j fixer 2). First refuse any env key that isn't a variable name (`^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$`): Claude Code 2.1.312 passes a key `"PATH=/dir:"` through as `PATH=/dir:=value` and finds the command on that PATH, so a whole-name list is bypassed by `NAME=` keys (901j verifier); it also passes keys with a space, an empty name, a line break, full-width or Cyrillic look-alike letters or `${…}` unchanged (no expansion in keys), and refuses to start a server with a NUL in an env key or value, an argument (`ERR_INVALID_ARG_VALUE`) or a remote server's header value, and refuses to connect when a header value holds an interior CR or LF (one at either end it trims with a whitespace warning and connects, as Node 22 fetch does; the trim is HTTP whitespace only, so a CR or LF next to a no-break space, U+000B or U+000C still counts as inside) or a character above U+00FF, or a header name is empty or holds a NUL, line break, space or colon (tab, U+0001, U+007F and Latin-1 values pass), while Codex 0.165 warns and connects without that header (any non-token name; CR, LF, NUL or any control character but tab in a value). Both send a template `Host` in place of the url's host, Codex sends a template `Content-Length` and cuts the body to it, Claude Code joins case-variant names into one header (two Bearer tokens in one `Authorization`) and expands `${NAME:-default}` (its pattern is `/\$\{([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?::-[^}]*)?)\}/g`: valid NAME and closing brace only, so `${1ABC}`, `${A.B}` and an unclosed `${A:-x` go as written; header values get a second pass, a variable's own value included, so `$${EMPTY}{SET}` with EMPTY empty reads SET, while command, args, env values and url get one; it also reads `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` and `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` as empty toward a remote server, set or unset (a stdio server gets the real value), a guaranteed empty reference: refuse `$` or an unfinished `${` directly before a reference, and `{` directly after one, in header values) in command, args, env, url and headers while Codex sends it as written. Node 22's own fetch, unlike Claude Code, also refuses U+0001–U+001F (not tab) and U+007F in a value, so Node-based clients (Cursor) may differ again. So a policy that reports a tool as verified checks all of these, not only NUL (901j fixer 4, verifier 3, fixer 6). To see what a launched server really received, have the fake command dump its own `/proc/$$/environ`: a `/bin/sh` fake re-exports only valid names, so odd keys look dropped (901j fixer 3) (901j review 2: Claude Code 2.1.312 ran a fake `npx` from env.PATH; `env -i HOME=<scratch> claude mcp add-json -s user …` then `claude mcp list` starts every user-scope stdio server, so a marker-writing fake command shows what ran, no login needed), and read its latest bin script from the tarball (download into an empty folder, check `dist.shasum`, never run it; npm `tsc` 2.0.4 calls itself a deprecated TypeScript release but its bin only prints a warning and exits 1), before calling it safe: the name can belong to someone else (npm `lhci` is a third-party placeholder with an `lhci` bin; Lighthouse CI is `@lhci/cli`) (9019, 2026-10-25).
